Ladybird now treats a moveBefore() out of or into a media element as a
removal or an insertion there. Blink does the same: Its state-preserving
move still runs HTMLSourceElement::RemovedFrom() and InsertedInto(),
which call HTMLMediaElement::SourceWasRemoved() and SourceWasAdded().
So does Gecko: Its nsINode::MoveBefore() calls RemoveChildNode() and
InsertChildBefore() with notifications, which reach the media element’s
ContentWillBeRemoved() and its source handling.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79 and Firefox 151.0, the new test loads the
source in both of its cases, as Ladybird now does too. WebKit 26.5 has
no moveBefore(), so it can’t run the test.

Moving a source into a video that has no src, and hasn’t started
resource selection, now starts it in Ladybird, as the spec’s source
moving steps ask. Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79 and Firefox 151.0, both
load the source; WebKit 26.5 has no moveBefore().
